#!/usr/bin/env bash
# E12-S3: the git guards the pages rely on are installed and required.
#   Part 1  the rules, pinned whole, unit by unit: preflight offers pre-commit in its one install ask; build step
#           41c switches the hooks on, makes the first save (gitleaks scans it) and moves the project to `work`;
#           Q12 is [BLOCK]; the catch-up (/resume-it) and /retrofit-it switch the hooks on; every skill that saves
#           for the user runs the same hook check first, saves nothing on HOOKS OFF, and reads the commit's exit
#           code; the skills that assume a branch after a build name `work`; the scaffolds' configs.
#   Part 2  the hook check itself, and /try-it's save line, run as written in throwaway repos
#   Part 3  REAL behaviour: a throwaway copy of the scaffold, step 41c run as written (twice), then: a token in
#           the first save stops it; a save on main is refused; a key in backend/tests/ or mock-services/ is
#           found; a .tsx and a .py save on `work` go through with Docker absent (the frontend checks print
#           their skip line) and, when Docker is here, with the frontend checks run in the container.
#           Needs pre-commit and the network (it fetches the hooks): without them it says SKIPPED loudly, and
#           fails in CI, where the workflow installs pre-commit.
# Keys are built at run time; no secret is written in this repository.
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
OLDROOT=$PWD
fail=0
ok()  { echo "ok    $*"; }
bad() { echo "FAIL  $*"; fail=1; }
T="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'chmod -R u+w "$T" 2>/dev/null; rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
MK=.claude/commands/make-it.md; GO=.claude/make-it/references/git-operations.md
SC=.claude/make-it/scaffolds/fastapi-nextjs; SF=.claude/make-it/scaffolds/nextjs-fullstack

# --- Part 1: the rules, pinned whole --------------------------------------------------------------
python3 - <<'PY' || fail=1
import re, sys
flat = lambda s: re.sub(r"\s+", " ", s).strip()
UNITS = [
    ('make-it preflight checks pre-commit', '.claude/commands/make-it.md', "pre-commit --version 2>/dev/null   # runs the project's checks before every save (prerequisites.md #6)"),
    ('prerequisites #6 pre-commit', '.claude/make-it/references/prerequisites.md', '### 6. pre-commit\n\n**Why needed:** Runs the project\'s checks before every save: gitleaks\' scan for passwords and keys, and `no-commit-to-branch`, which stops a save straight to `main`. /make-it switches them on in every build (check Q12), and the skills that save for the user save nothing while they are off.\n**How to check:** `pre-commit --version 2>/dev/null`\n**What to tell the user it is:** "pre-commit -- runs your project\'s checks before every save: a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy"\n**If missing:** it is a Quick Fix, offered in preflight\'s one install ask with the other missing tools (a pick: install it, walk them through it, or their company\'s way), never installed without it.\n- macOS: `brew install pre-commit`\n- Windows: `py -m pip install --user pre-commit`\n- Linux: `pipx install pre-commit` or `python3 -m pip install --user pre-commit`'),
    ('prerequisites: the instant check', '.claude/make-it/references/prerequisites.md', '- pre-commit installed? `pre-commit --version 2>/dev/null`'),
    ('prerequisites: a quick fix', '.claude/make-it/references/prerequisites.md', '- Examples: Docker, GitHub CLI, VS Code, pre-commit'),
    ('make-it step b points at 41c and work', '.claude/commands/make-it.md', 'Step 41c switches these hooks on (`pre-commit install`), makes the first save, and moves the\n      project to its working branch, `work`. After that first save, all work goes on `work` or a\n      worktree branched from it (`scripts/worktree.sh new <branch>`), never on `main`/`master`.'),
    ('make-it step 41c', '.claude/commands/make-it.md', '41c. **Switch on the checks before every save, save the build, and move to `work` (silent -- checks Q12, VC02).** The hooks go on first, so the first save is checked too: gitleaks scans everything it saves. That one save skips only `no-commit-to-branch` (it is the save that belongs on the default branch) and the two frontend checks that run in Docker. Then the project moves to a working branch, `work`, so every later save goes there and the default branch stays as the build left it until /ship-it. Before it, run `git status --short` and check that nothing secret-looking is about to be saved (never save a `.env` or key file; the scaffold\'s `.gitignore` leaves them out). Then, in the project (safe to run again): ```bash pre-commit install || { echo "HOOKS NOT ON"; exit 1; } hk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)" [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk" || { echo "HOOKS NOT ON"; exit 1; } if ! git rev-parse -q --verify HEAD >/dev/null; then git add -A && SKIP=no-commit-to-branch,eslint,prettier git commit -q -m "Initial build" || { echo "FIRST SAVE REFUSED"; exit 1; } fi [ "$(git branch --show-current)" = work ] || git switch -q work 2>/dev/null || git switch -q -c work || { echo "NO WORK BRANCH"; exit 1; } echo "HOOKS ON" ``` It must print `HOOKS ON` (Q12 is [BLOCK]). Otherwise fix the cause and run it again: - `HOOKS NOT ON`: `pre-commit install` printed why above it. `pre-commit` missing: preflight\'s install ask for it. Another tool already manages this project\'s git hooks (`core.hooksPath` is set): stop before /try-it and tell the user: "I couldn\'t switch on the checks your project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy), so I\'ve stopped before showing you the app. [the reason, in plain words]" - `FIRST SAVE REFUSED`: a hook that tidies files (trailing spaces, line endings, ruff\'s fixes) changed some -- run it again and they are saved; gitleaks found a value that looks like a password or key (its output names it: a `RuleID:` line, `leaks found: 1` or more) -- move it into `.env` (as the security step does), then run it again; gitleaks stopped without naming one (a `panic:`, a stack trace, killed, `no leaks found in partial scan`) or couldn\'t be set up -- it couldn\'t check, so nothing is saved: run it again, and if it keeps crashing, stop before /try-it and tell the user the "couldn\'t check" line (`git-operations.md`, After the save: A crash is not a finding); git doesn\'t know the user\'s name and email -- ask for them; git couldn\'t sign the save (`commit.gpgsign` is on, but no signing key works here) -- show the user git\'s own line above it, and ask them to fix signing or turn it off for this project (`git config commit.gpgsign false`). Then tell the user, in one line: "Your project\'s saves go on a working copy called \'work\'; the main copy stays clean until you ship."'),
    ('make-it state file records the branch', '.claude/commands/make-it.md', 'Working branch: `work` (every save goes here; `main` holds the first build until /ship-it).'),
    ('Q12 [BLOCK]', '.claude/make-it/references/build-standards.md', "**Q12** [Tier 0] [BLOCK] **Pre-commit hooks installed locally** -- When `.pre-commit-config.yaml` exists, the file git runs before each commit (`git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit`) exists, can be run, and is pre-commit's own: it holds `File generated by pre-commit`. /make-it runs `pre-commit install` before the build's first save (build-verify step 41c, which then moves the project to its `work` branch), so in its first build-verify the check is made there, not in Part A. /resume-it's catch-up and /retrofit-it switch it on when it is off (one plain line), asking preflight's install pick for `pre-commit` first when it is missing. /wrap-it, /try-it, /argo-it, /fix-it, /resume-it, /retrofit-it and /deadcode-it run the same check before every save they make for the user and save nothing while it fails (`git-operations.md`, Before any save); the one save built with the hooks off by design is /argo-it's first-deploy save, which holds only argo-it's files and is checked by its own secret scan. CI does not depend on pre-commit hooks."),
    ('Q08 gitleaks rules and allowlist', '.claude/make-it/references/build-standards.md', "**Q08** [Tier 0] [FIX] **Secret detection configured (gitleaks)** -- `.gitleaks.toml` exists at project root. gitleaks hook is in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. The allowlist names only known mock values (the mock-token patterns) and exact files (`.env.example`, the mock seed script), each path anchored -- never a whole folder such as `tests/` or `mock-services/`, which would hide a real key put there. The file extends gitleaks' default rules (`[extend]` with `useDefault = true`): a config that only lists allowlists has no rules, and gitleaks finds nothing."),
    ('git-operations: Before any save, After the save, The branch', '.claude/make-it/references/git-operations.md', '### Before any save\n\nA save a skill makes for the user never goes ahead with the project\'s hooks switched off. Before\nit, run:\n\n```bash\nhk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || echo "HOOKS OFF"\n```\n\nIt prints `HOOKS OFF` when the project has a `.pre-commit-config.yaml` but git has no working\npre-commit hook to run (check Q12). Then nothing is saved, and the user hears: "I haven\'t saved\nanything: the checks this project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop\non saving straight to the main copy) are switched off on this computer. Run /resume-it to switch\nthem on." A project with no `.pre-commit-config.yaml` saves as before. Never get past it with\n`--no-verify`. The build\'s first save (/make-it step 41c) and /resume-it\'s Desktop hand-off are\nmade by the step that switches the hooks on. One save is built with the hooks off by design:\n/argo-it\'s first-deploy save (PHASE 5), which holds only argo-it\'s own files and is checked by its\nown secret scan before anything is sent.\n\n**After the save**, read `git commit`\'s exit code (never throw it away with `2>/dev/null` or\n`|| true`). Anything but 0 means nothing was saved: never say it was. Tell the user, from what the\nhook printed: "I couldn\'t save your changes: [what stopped it, in plain words -- the scan for\npasswords and keys found one in [file], line [N] / it would have saved straight to the main copy /\na check of the code failed in [file]]. They\'re still in your files, unsaved. [what to do next, in\nplain words -- for the main copy: "Say \'move my work to a working copy\' and I\'ll do it."]" -- never\nthe value itself.'),
    ('git-operations: The branch', '.claude/make-it/references/git-operations.md', '**The branch.** A /make-it project works on `work`: step 41c moves it there after the first save,\nso every save a skill makes lands on `work` (or a worktree branched from it), and `main` holds the\nfirst build until /ship-it sends `work` in for review. A project /make-it didn\'t build (one\n/retrofit-it upgrades, say) keeps its own branches.\n\n**Move to `work`.** An app built before step 41c existed still works on the default branch, where\n`no-commit-to-branch` refuses every save once its hooks are on. Any skill moves it, in the project,\nwhen /resume-it\'s catch-up has just switched the hooks on on the default branch, or when the user\nasks to move their work to a working copy (in those words or any that mean it, or by picking it):\n\n```bash\nb=$(git branch --show-current)\ncase $b in\n  main|master)\n    if git show-ref -q --verify refs/heads/work; then\n      git merge-base --is-ancestor "$b" work || { echo "WORK DIFFERS"; exit 0; }\n      git switch -q work || { echo "SWITCH FAILED"; exit 0; }\n    else\n      git switch -q -c work || { echo "SWITCH FAILED"; exit 0; }\n    fi\n    echo "MOVED" ;;\nesac\n```\n\nUnsaved changes travel with the switch. On `MOVED`, say the line step 41c says: "Your project\'s\nsaves go on a working copy called \'work\'; the main copy stays clean until you ship." On `WORK\nDIFFERS`: "Your project already has a working copy called \'work\' with saves that aren\'t in the main\ncopy, so I\'ve left you on the main copy. A developer can bring the two together." On `SWITCH\nFAILED`: "I couldn\'t move your work to the working copy \'work\': [git\'s reason, in plain words]. I\nhaven\'t changed anything." It prints nothing when the project isn\'t on the default branch.\n\n**Bring `work` level after a ship.** Once a pull request from `work` is merged (squash-merged, and\nthe branch deleted on GitHub, as usual), the next session brings the local `work` level with the\ndefault branch, at /resume-it\'s session start, every time:\n\n```bash\n[ "$(git branch --show-current)" = work ] && git fetch -q origin 2>/dev/null || exit 0\nd=$(git symbolic-ref -q --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null); d=${d:-origin/main}\ngit rev-parse -q --verify "$d" >/dev/null || exit 0\nif git merge-base --is-ancestor work "$d"; then\n  [ "$(git rev-parse work)" = "$(git rev-parse "$d")" ] || { git merge -q --ff-only "$d" && echo "BROUGHT LEVEL" || echo "NOT LEVELLED"; }\nelif [ "$(git merge-tree --write-tree "$d" work 2>/dev/null | head -1)" = "$(git rev-parse "$d^{tree}")" ]; then\n  git reset -q --keep "$d" && echo "BROUGHT LEVEL" || echo "NOT LEVELLED"\nelse\n  echo "UNSHIPPED SAVES"\nfi\n```\n\n`work` restarts from the default branch\'s head only when everything it holds is already there (a\nmerged pull request); unsaved changes are kept (`--ff-only` and `--keep` refuse rather than\noverwrite them). On `BROUGHT LEVEL`, say: "Your shipped changes are in the main copy now, so your\nworking copy is up to date with it." On `UNSHIPPED SAVES`, say nothing unless the user is about to\nship: `work` holds saves that aren\'t in the main copy yet, and it is left as it is. On `NOT\nLEVELLED`: "Your working copy couldn\'t be brought up to date with the main copy, because your\nunsaved changes touch the same files. Nothing was changed."'),
    ('git-operations: the shipped hook', '.claude/make-it/references/git-operations.md', "make-it's installers add a Claude Code hook that refuses `git push --force`, `-f`, `--mirror`\n  and `+refspec` pushes (`~/.claude/make-it/hooks/no-force-push.sh`) and lets `--force-with-lease`\n  through."),
    ('worktree-workflow: /make-it moves to work', '.claude/make-it/references/worktree-workflow.md', '- **/make-it** — scaffolds `scripts/worktree.sh`, the `no-commit-to-branch` hook, and the\n  parameterized compose into every project; step 41c switches the hooks on, makes the first save\n  on the default branch, and moves the project to its working branch, `work`. Feature work goes\n  on `work` or a worktree branched from it; `main` stays as the build left it until /ship-it.'),
    ('worktree-workflow: /ship-it ships work', '.claude/make-it/references/worktree-workflow.md', "- **/ship-it** — ships the current worktree's branch (`work` after a build); the default branch stays clean."),
    ('ship-it-guide: the branch it sends', '.claude/make-it/references/ship-it-guide.md', "**The branch it sends.** A /make-it project is on `work` when /ship-it runs: the build's step 41c\nmade the first save on `main` and moved every later save to `work`. So /ship-it sends `work` (or\nthe branch of the worktree it runs in) as the pull request's branch, into `main`; it never commits\nto `main` (the project's `no-commit-to-branch` hook refuses that) and never pushes `main` past the\nfirst build. On the first ship, GitHub has no `main` yet: /ship-it has to push `main` as the build\nleft it, then open the pull request from `work`."),
    ('resume-it Desktop a ends on work', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', 'then save the\n  bundle as /make-it\'s step 41c does: with a `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, run that step\'s block as\n  written (hooks on, the first save on the default branch, then `work`); without one,\n  `git add -A && git commit -m "Initial build" && git switch -c work`. Either way it ends on\n  `work`, and all further work goes there (WORKTREE FIRST, below).'),
