    ('resume-it worktree-first branches from work', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', '`scripts/worktree.sh new <branch>` (it branches from where you are: `work` in a project /make-it\nbuilt, so `main` stays as the build left it until /ship-it).'),
    ('resume-it catch-up Q12', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', '- **Hooks on (Q12):** when `.pre-commit-config.yaml` exists and the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save) prints `HOOKS OFF`: if `pre-commit` is installed, run `pre-commit install` now -- routine, no ask -- and say one line: "I switched on the checks your project runs before every save: a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy." If `pre-commit` isn\'t installed, ask preflight\'s install pick for it (`make-it.md` preflight, the one ask for missing tools), then switch the hooks on the same way. If `pre-commit install` fails, say why in plain words (another tool already manages this project\'s git hooks, for example) and that the checks stay off. Once they are on, if the project is on its default branch, run "Move to `work`" (`git-operations.md`, The branch) and say its line. Q12 never goes in the gaps list or its ask.'),
    ('resume-it save', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', 'Before that commit, run the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save):\n`hk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || echo "HOOKS OFF"`.\nIf it prints `HOOKS OFF` (the catch-up couldn\'t switch the hooks on, or the user turned down\ninstalling pre-commit), commit nothing and say: "I haven\'t saved anything: the checks this project\nruns before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main\ncopy) are switched off on this computer. Say \'switch on the save checks\' and I\'ll set them up." After\nthe commit, read its exit code: anything but 0 means nothing was saved -- never say it was; say the\n"couldn\'t save" line (`git-operations.md`, After the save).'),
    ('retrofit Verify Q12', '.claude/commands/retrofit-it.md', "- **Q01-Q16**: Code quality (Q14, the dead-code upkeep reminder, is N/A right after a retrofit; Q12 is switched on the way /resume-it's catch-up does it, with its one plain line)"),
    ('retrofit snapshot Q12, no work branch', '.claude/commands/retrofit-it.md', '**Before the snapshot, the hooks (Q12):** when the app has its own repo and a\n`.pre-commit-config.yaml`, run the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save). On `HOOKS\nOFF`, switch the hooks on the way /resume-it\'s catch-up does (Q12: `pre-commit install` and its one\nplain line, preflight\'s install pick first when `pre-commit` is missing), then run the check again.\nStill `HOOKS OFF`: stop here, before any change, and tell the user: "I haven\'t changed or saved\nanything: the checks this project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop\non saving straight to the main copy) are switched off on this computer. [when `pre-commit install`\nfailed: the reason, in plain words / otherwise: Run /retrofit-it again and pick Install when I ask\nabout pre-commit.]" A folder that wasn\'t under git yet gets the same step right after the\nsnapshot, before Phase 5: its snapshot is its first save, the one `pre-commit install` follows.\nUnlike /make-it\'s step 41c, nothing here moves the app to a `work` branch: an existing repo keeps\nits own branches, and Phase 5 makes the retrofit\'s own branch as it always has.'),
    ('wrap-it save: the hook check', '.claude/commands/wrap-it.md', '- Before staging anything, run the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save):\n  `hk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || echo "HOOKS OFF"`. If it prints `HOOKS OFF`, save nothing and say, in place of the save\n  line: "I haven\'t saved anything: the checks this project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy) are switched off on this computer. Run /resume-it to switch them on." The rest of the wrap-up goes on.'),
    ('wrap-it save: the exit code', '.claude/commands/wrap-it.md', '- Read the commit\'s exit code. Anything but 0 means nothing was saved (a hook refused it): never\n  say "Saved your changes". Say instead: "I couldn\'t save your changes: [what stopped it, in plain\n  words, from what the hook printed -- the scan for passwords and keys found one in [file], line\n  [N] / it would have saved straight to the main copy / a check of the code failed in [file]].\n  They\'re still on this computer, unsaved, so nothing is lost. [what to do next, in plain words --\n  for the main copy: "Say \'move my work to a working copy\' and I\'ll do it."]"'),
    ('try-it save: exit code and hook check', '.claude/commands/try-it.md', '- Unless an update is unfinished (above), stage and commit any fixes: `git add -A -- . \':(exclude).ship-it-go-live.json\' && { git diff --cached --quiet || git commit -m "Apply safety patches" -- . \':(exclude).ship-it-go-live.json\'; }`\n  (after a /make-it build this lands on `work`). If it exits with anything but 0, nothing was saved:\n  never say the patches were saved; when the app is presented, say the "couldn\'t save" line\n  (`git-operations.md`, After the save), filled from what the hook printed.\n- Before that save, run the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save): `hk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || echo "HOOKS OFF"`.\n  If it prints `HOOKS OFF`, commit nothing, and when the app is presented say: "I haven\'t saved anything: the checks this project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy) are switched off on this computer. Run /resume-it to switch them on."'),
    ('argo-it 5b step 4 hook check', '.claude/commands/argo-it.md', '# The project\'s checks before every save must be on: a .pre-commit-config.yaml and no pre-commit hook saves nothing\nhk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || { echo "HOOKS OFF"; exit 3; }'),
    ('argo-it HOOKS OFF rule', '.claude/commands/argo-it.md', '**If the block prints `HOOKS OFF`, stop here -- nothing was saved or sent.** The project has a\n`.pre-commit-config.yaml`, but git has no pre-commit hook to run (`git-operations.md`, Before any\nsave). Tell the user: "I haven\'t saved or sent anything: the checks this project runs before every\nsave (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy) are switched\noff on this computer. What to do next: run /resume-it to switch them on, then run /argo-it again."'),
    ('fix-it saves', '.claude/commands/fix-it.md', '**Before any commit in Steps 7-9**, run the hook check (`git-operations.md`, Before any save):\n`hk="$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit)"; [ ! -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.pre-commit-config.yaml" ] || { [ -x "$hk" ] && grep -qs \'File generated by pre-commit\' "$hk"; } || echo "HOOKS OFF"`. If it prints `HOOKS OFF`, commit nothing: the fixes stay in the files,\nunsaved, and the report opens with: "I haven\'t saved anything: the checks this project runs before every save (a scan for passwords and keys, and a stop on saving straight to the main copy) are switched off on this computer. Run /resume-it to switch them on." After each commit, read its exit code: anything but 0 means\nthat finding\'s fix wasn\'t saved -- say so in its report line with the "couldn\'t save" line\n(`git-operations.md`, After the save), never "closed".'),
    ('deadcode-it precondition points at the check', '.claude/commands/deadcode-it.md', '(after the hook check in `delete-safe`; one commit of exactly those files on the current branch, message `chore: save work before'),
    ('ship-it-guide: what the plugin does today', '.claude/make-it/references/ship-it-guide.md', 'What the /ship-it plugin does today (it is a separate plugin, not changed with make-it; read from\nits SKILL.md): in ship mode it stays on a branch that isn\'t `main` ("If already on a feature\nbranch: stay on it"), commits there and runs `git push -u origin {branch}`, so it sends `work`. It\ndoes not push `main` first, so on a repository whose GitHub copy has no `main` yet, the pull\nrequest has no `main` to go into: until the plugin does that step, push `main` once by hand\n(`git push origin main`) before the first ship. Its save mode makes a `wip/...` branch from\n`work`. Its docai/VLF production mode requires `main` and pushes to it; it is not for /make-it\napps.'),
    ('ship-it-guide: work after the merge', '.claude/make-it/references/ship-it-guide.md', 'After the pull request from `work` is merged (squash-merged, and the branch deleted on GitHub, as\nusual), the next session brings the local `work` level with the default branch: /resume-it runs\n"Bring `work` level after a ship" (`git-operations.md`, The branch) at its start.'),
    ('resume-it 0a2 brings work level', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', '**0a2. Bring `work` level (fixed check, every session):** run "Bring `work` level after a ship"\n(`git-operations.md`, The branch) and say its line, if it gives one. When the user asks to move\ntheir work to a working copy (in those words or any that mean it), run "Move to `work`" from the\nsame section.'),
    ('resume-it suggests the move', '.claude/commands/resume-it.md', '| On the default branch with the save checks on | "Your saves go to the main copy, which refuses them. Move your work to a working copy called \'work\'?" (picked: "Move to `work`", `git-operations.md`) |'),
